# Which key signs which Abla releases. tools/verify-release.sh --version picks the key here, and setup-abla's scripts/setup.sh
# carries the same rows. A row is `FIRST LAST KEY`: releases FIRST to LAST (versions without the v; LAST `-` while the key
# still signs) are signed by KEY, an ssh public key line. Ranges never overlap, and FIRST of the first row is
# tools/first-signed-release. The open row's key is release-signing.pub, the key the release workflow signs with
# (tools/test-release-signing.sh checks both).
# Rotation: set the old row's LAST to the last release it signed, add the new key from the next version with LAST `-`,
# replace release-signing.pub and the release environment's secret, and add the same rows to setup-abla. A release keeps
# verifying under the key of its own range only: an old key cannot sign a later release, and a new key cannot re-sign an old one.
